module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.subject_and_its_digest_as_independent_parameters

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data subject_and_its_digest_as_independent_parameters: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "subject_and_its_digest_as_independent_parameters" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a value and a summary of it -- a digest, a count, a view, an annotation -- placed side by side without a join can disagree silently, and the corroboration the reader believes they are getting was never computed** (INVALID STATE: a signature takes a value AND a summary of that value as two peer arguments -- `envelope` beside `envelope_digest`, `roster_identities` beside `roster_digest` -- with no arm relating them. The signature admits a digest of something OTHER than the value beside it; nothing refuses; and every downstream guarantee that cites the digest is then citing a summary of a different object, while reading exactly like a guarantee about the value. DISTINGUISHING FACTS: the two arguments are supplied by the same caller from the same site TODAY, so the pairing is held by lexical proximity rather than by anything the type system or a fold can see; the defect is constructible without touching a single caller, by supplying a correct digest of a different subject. EVIDENCE, TWO INSTANCES IN ONE MODULE, which is what makes it a class rather than a coincidence: `v2.workflow.floor_wet_route` `wet_route_gate_disposition_for_receipt` carries BOTH the envelope/digest pair and the roster-identities/roster-digest pair. The first was demonstrated live -- `v2.test.floor_wet_route` `wet_lease_admits_an_envelope_whose_content_the_digest_does_not_name` constructs an envelope whose content the pinned digest does not name, and the lease gate ADMITS. THE SAME DEFECT READ FROM THE OTHER END: agreement among derived views is vacuous when the views share one reader. A projection was nearly shipped with a row silently deleted because FOUR derived views agreed, all fed by a single reader -- one source feeding many views made agreement carry no information, exactly as a digest and its subject being independent lets them disagree unnoticed. THE THIRD FACE, AND THE ONE THAT SURVIVED REVIEW LONGEST: PROSE ADJACENCY IS NOT A JOIN EITHER. `wet_route_identity_rows_block_with_publication` carried an annotation stating that EXACTLY TWO per-identity classes are waived under a valid publication transaction, three lines above a body that returned false unconditionally and waived FOUR -- so `no_verdict`, the ABSENCE of a verdict, was converted into a green merge path. The prose and the code disagreed by a factor of two IN THE SAME FUNCTION and FOUR APPROVALS READ PAST IT. An annotation is not checked against the body it sits above by anything: it READS as documentation and FUNCTIONS as an assertion, and by DESIGN section 4c no `Accepted` program can read one, so nothing in the toolchain will ever disagree with it. Provider count is not attention -- four approvals is not four readings of that function. AND FIELD ADJACENCY IS NOT A JOIN EITHER, which is the same claim one scale down: in `v1_compiler.bin.claim_batch`'s two pre-execution refusal arms the annotation two lines above `WetReceiptIdentityRow` was RIGHT, and the neighbouring fields `observed_entry_rel` and `observed_function` were RIGHT -- Optional, and correctly None on exactly those arms -- and `wall_ms`, two fields later in the SAME STRUCT LITERAL, fabricates a 0 for a duration nothing measured. PROXIMITY TO A CORRECT THING IS NOT CORRECTNESS: a reader scanning that literal gets three signals of care and one fabrication, with nothing in the text distinguishing them. And 0 is the WORST available fabricated value for that field rather than a neutral placeholder, because it is THE FASTEST OBSERVABLE EXECUTION -- so a consumer that averages, thresholds or ranks on wall_ms without first branching on the outcome wire reads the ABSENCE of a measurement as the BEST measurement in the corpus. An anomalous-looking fabrication is found by whoever first sorts the column; this one sorts to the top and is found by nobody. All three faces are one defect: A JOIN THAT WAS NEVER MADE, so the corroboration a reader believes they are getting was never computed. RECOGNITION RULE: whenever a parameter list contains a value and a summary, hash, count, digest or identity OF that value, ask what refuses when they disagree. If the answer is nothing, the summary is unverified input wearing the costume of a derived fact. The mirror question for agreement: when N sources agree, ask how many INDEPENDENT reads produced them; if the answer is one, the agreement is a copy agreeing with itself. THE REPAIR, already built once and therefore not speculative: DERIVE THE DIGEST FROM THE VALUE AT THE POINT THE VALUE IS OBTAINED, NEVER ACCEPT IT AS A PEER PARAMETER. The seed`s `read_wet_receipt_envelope` now returns the decoded envelope and the digest of the bytes it decoded FROM, in one call, so the two cannot name different objects; its control `carried_digest_names_the_bytes_the_envelope_was_decoded_from` rewrites the file between reads and asserts the carried digest still names its own bytes. RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable -- a comment and a caller convention. CEILING: structurally impossible, because a digest produced by the same operation that produces the value has no constructor that lets it name a different one. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a capability: a carrier type that pairs a value with a digest DERIVED at construction, so no signature anywhere can accept the two separately -- SUFFICIENT FOR every remaining declaration that takes a value and a digest of that value as peer parameters. NOT retired by `gunbc.guarantee_stall` `wet_route_model_lags_seed_stall` as a whole: that recovered row (gunbc#9725 commit 32815827ef8) names three members spanning two classes, so repairing only a content-digest member would leave peer-parameter signatures writable (DESIGN section 4b(3)).)",
    "**THE ROW'S DECLARED REPAIR IS CONSTRUCTIBLE ONLY WHERE THE SUBJECT BYTES REACH A REALIZATION, AND THE TWO DIGEST SHAPES THAT LOOK ALIKE ARE NOT ALIKE.** This row declares the repair as already built once -- the seed's `read_wet_receipt_envelope` returns the decoded envelope and the digest of the bytes it decoded FROM in one call. Asking what it takes to apply that in `.dag` separates two shapes a name search conflates. `extdeps.crypto.hash` `sha256_digest` takes HEX: an author asserts a digest and nothing computes it, which is this class in its pure form. `extdeps.tools.sha256sum` `sha256sum_file_digest_via_shell` takes a PATH and the digest is COMPUTED over the bytes at that path, with a typed `Sha256FileDigestUnavailable` arm; under DESIGN section 3 that is a shell HANDLER bound to a digest shape, one of N, and it is live in `gunbc.instruments.fabric_control_plane_live_probe`. A computed digest at a host-tool-dependent rung is NOT an assertion, and an earlier draft of this receipt wrongly folded them together and claimed every digest in the corpus is asserted or verified out-of-band. It is not; that claim is withdrawn. WHAT SURVIVES IS NARROWER AND IS THE ACTUAL PRECONDITION: no `.dag` function takes `std.bytes` `Bytes` and returns a digest, so the repair is reachable exactly when the subject bytes EXIST AT A PATH and unreachable when they exist only in memory. That is a question about the realization, not about the class, and it must be answered per instance rather than defaulted. INSTANCE (fabric-m0, gunbc#10641): `gunbc.fabric_m0_completion` carries `manifest_digest` beside `members` on `CommitManifest`, and an `OriginObservation`'s digest is asserted rather than computed over the bytes read back, so a real readback digest paired with an arbitrary authored member set reads as a valid committed manifest. ANSWERED EXPLICITLY: this instance is NOT blocked. Its subjects are artifacts, receipts and a commit manifest -- stored objects -- and a durable-origin readback that stages what it fetched puts those bytes at a path, so the shell handler computes over exactly them and the chain is buildable at a mitigatable rung, with the in-substrate primitive as the CLIMB rather than the prerequisite. THE CONSTRAINT THAT REALIZATION MUST RESPECT, and it is specific: the digested file must be the ORIGIN readback's own output and never a cache copy, or the computation is a byte-exact confirmation of the wrong provenance -- the cache-read arm `gunbc.fabric_m0_commit` `manifest_readback_for_slot` already refuses. A computed digest over locally-sourced bytes would launder that refusal into a confirmation. THE ADJACENT DEFECT, REPAIRED, and it is a DIFFERENT one: the readback was a FIELD of every committed row, so a readback existed for every object by construction and 'was this read at all' had no representation. Reads are now a separate population joined by the authority, a subject lacking an observation resolves to undetermined, and seeding that case to satisfied reddens exactly the two witnesses written for it. That closes absence-of-a-read and does not close digest-to-bytes. THE HALF-REPAIR WAS DECLINED DELIBERATELY: a carrier holding bytes plus an ASSERTED digest plus decoded members relocates the fabrication to one boundary while simulating the byte layer, and reads as progress in review. The stall named as absent in an earlier draft of this closing is restored; this class's trigger is not that stall as a whole (DESIGN section 4b(3)), recorded in the following receipt.",
    "**THE STALL `wet_route_model_lags_seed_stall` IS RESTORED, AND THIS CLASS'S TRIGGER IS NOT THAT STALL AS A WHOLE (silent-badger-818, 2026-09-07).** Restored from commit `32815827ef8` on gunbc#9725 (recovered, not re-derived). The FIRST receipt's next-rung trigger does not retire against that stall as a whole -- the recovered population spans two classes, so repairing only a content-digest member must not satisfy a peer-parameter trigger (DESIGN section 4b(3)). `self_host_wet_route_receipt_lifetime_stall` is about route families and receipt lifetime and is NOT this population; a tidy section-3 retarget would invent the join this class files. The fabric-m0 instance in the preceding receipt is unchanged.)",
    "**FILE-GRAIN ADJACENCY IS THE NEXT FACE OF THE SAME CLASS, NOT A PARALLEL CLASS.** The first receipt numbers three faces and then records field adjacency as the same claim one scale down, closing all three as one defect. Counting that scale as its own face, this is the fifth: a `data live_tree_disposition` row beside the body that actually calls. THE RECOGNITION RULE STILL READS: a value and a summary of it held together by nothing a fold can see. The original rule's 'parameter list' sentence is the specimen grain of the first faces, not a restriction of the class; the stamp is a summary of live-checkout reach and the body is the value, and `parse_entry_live_tree_disposition` never looks at the body. A second row for this grain would be a meaning fork of this identity. INVALID STATE: an entry declares SubstrateInputsOnly while its unquoted body calls a live-checkout sink (callee spellings from `v1_compiler.cli_run.floor_skip_frontier_tests` `direct_live_tree_sink_callees`, derived at census time from `v1.compiler.method` `builtin_function_registry` by `builtin_name_spells_a_live_checkout_sink`, plus the method surfaces `Filesystem.Read` / `Filesystem.List` which are not registry keys). CONFORMANCE (DESIGN section 3b), STATED: substring matching rather than `v2.std.fn_index` `callees_from_node` CONFORMS to the model this fact already lives in, it does not diverge. The census must read entries that may not RESOLVE; a text parser reaches those files and a Node fold does not; `parse_entry_live_tree_disposition` is already a textual seed reader on the same line, so the detector inhabits that reader rather than the resolved-call fold. This is not that the fold was harder. STALENESS, MADE VISIBLE: a live-checkout builtin whose name fails `builtin_name_spells_a_live_checkout_sink` is silent here -- the roster does not know, nothing in this detector refuses, and the census under-reports. The residual transcription is the PREDICATE, not a pasted name list; a new `compile_dag_*` key in the registry is picked up. What would refuse a name the predicate misses is `call_reachability_live_read_classification`, not this instrument. MECHANISM, AND IT IS BIGGER THAN ANY ONE PROBE'S AUTHOR: `reads_live_tree_effective` consults effect_reach only after a SubstrateInputsOnly stamp, and effect_reach's host-sink roster (`v2.std.effect_reach` `effect_reach_host_sink_callee_symbols_v0` / `EFFECT_REACH_HOST_SINK_MARKERS`) does not contain `compile_dag_diagnostic_census` -- so a lying SubstrateInputsOnly is NOT upgraded and IS skip-eligible on an outside-closure diff. `HostEffectSinkKind` already names `GunbcCompileEntrySink`, and the v0 roster still has no `compile_dag_*` symbol; the hole is marker coverage, not a missing kind. Executed under `cargo test --release -p v1-compiler --lib` (`v1_compiler.cli_run.floor_skip_frontier_tests` `compile_dag_diagnostic_census_is_invisible_to_effect_reach_host_sinks`). That command is local diligence, not a required CI step -- `gunbc.rung_drop` `rust_unit_tests_off_the_merge_path`. It is not merge-path evidence. WHETHER ADDING THE MARKER IS THE CHEAPER NEXT RUNG -- CHECKED, NOT HUNCHED: it is cheaper in authoring (one symbol plus the Rust mirror the drift gate already joins). It is not the next rung. The derivation conjoins TWO independent existentials (path-like `data` String starting with dag/ or src/ and containing .dag, AND a host-sink substring) over an import-adjacent closure. The remaining SIO+census specimen `test.claim.citation_cause_subject_disjointness_witness` compiles in-memory source strings; its String rows contain spaces and are not path literals. `adding_the_census_marker_would_still_lose_the_conjunction_on_the_remaining_specimen` holds both terms false on that file, which is the same ceiling `effect_reach_conjunction_loses_a_closure_that_reads_with_no_path_literal` already plants. `v2.std.effect_reach` `effect_reach_detection_ceiling` already forbids patching any of its three bounds: a function wrong in both directions is replaced, not patched, and a roster repair would leave the approach standing while making it look sounder than it is; its ceiling stays mitigatable; all three bounds derive `call_reachability_live_read_classification`. Filling the census hole would satisfy a weaker sentence while that capability stayed dead (DESIGN section 4b(3)). THE TRIGGER IS THEREFORE UNCHANGED. HARM: the stamp is a lie that is skip-eligible and has no measured miss on record. The mechanism that would have shown such a drop, falsifier.yml, was itself deleted 2026-08-15, so no round after that date could have shown one. That is not a claim that falsifier.yml itself dropped one. Honest, not a guess. Import-adjacency over the same sinks is not this face: it over-approximates. ReadsLiveTree entries whose unquoted body matches no name on this detector's list are UNDETECTED-BY-THIS-INSTRUMENT, not a disagreement set -- the detector already produced a quoted false-positive in `dag/std/content_hash.dag` before strings were stripped. SPECIMENS identity-joined, not counted: the planted triple `detector_hits_planted_triple_is_an_identity_join`; remaining live SIO+call via `named_remaining_sio_direct_live_specimen_is_joined`. Individually established honest restamps of named census probes are not a batch of hand-stamped facts; withholding the rest is because that set is such a batch, and the derivation that makes it correct at once is already the named capability. THE CORPUS NOTE FORBIDS RETYPING A DECLARATION TO FIT A STAMP, not correcting a stamp to match a body. RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. CEILING: structurally impossible once disposition is constructed from the closure. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER is the capability already declared at `v2.std.effect_reach` `call_reachability_live_read_classification` -- cite that row, do not restate it. `std.artifact_store_scratch_grant`'s dissolve-on sentence names envelope-derived LiveTreeDisposition as what retires hand-stamped ReadsLiveTree; that is this same capability, not a second trigger.)",
    "**THE FABRIC-M0 INSTANCE IS REPAIRED, AND THE REPAIR SPLITS THE CLASS INTO A SUB-CLASS AT RUNG 4 AND AN ENCLOSING ONE STILL AT RUNG 1 (lively-ibex-812).** The preceding receipt named the fabric-m0 instance NOT BLOCKED and stated the constraint its realization must respect: the digested file must be the ORIGIN readback own output and never a cache copy, or a correct computation over the wrong provenance launders the cache-read refusal into a confirmation. WHAT WAS ACTUALLY THERE was worse than an asserted digest. `gunbc.fabric_m0_commit` carried `ManifestRead { observed_digest, source: ReadSource }` -- the digest AND a provenance STAMP as two peer values -- and the fold refused the `DisposableCacheRead` arm. That is validation standing exactly where construction was available, and it failed its own review tell: the refusal was satisfied by EDITING THE DECLARATION, writing `DurableOriginRead` in place of `DisposableCacheRead`, while the realization still digested a local copy. THE REPAIR IS THIS ROW OWN: `gunbc.fabric_m0_origin_readback` `OriginReading` is a sole_constructor RECORD pairing a staged path with the digest computed over it by one operation, `read_origin_staged_file`; `ManifestRead` and `ReadbackConfirmed` now CARRY that reading instead of a digest; `ReadSource`, `DurableOriginRead` and `DisposableCacheRead` are DELETED rather than kept beside it, and the fold arm that refused the cache dissolves with them, its witness re-shaped and still enrolled in the required floor (DESIGN section 4b(4)). A cache read reaches `cache_manifest_read`, whose only product is a located refusal. THE SEAL IS ON A RECORD FOR A MEASURED REASON, and this is the reusable finding: sole_constructor on a COPRODUCT does not refuse cross-module variant construction -- the hole measured by `test.claim.sole_constructor_completeness_audit_probe` `f13_variant_construction_refuses`, which is red -- so sealing `ManifestReadbackObservation` or `OriginReadback` directly would have been a permanently green wall, worse than absent. Sealing the record the confirming variants CARRY puts the forbidden literal back on the form the wall does refuse. Verified by compiling both shapes: a sealed-record literal cross-module refuses `SoleConstructorViolation`, the sealed-coproduct variant compiles with 0 diagnostics. AN ENUMERATED ADMISSION LIST WAS TRIED FIRST AND WAS A HOLE, which is the reusable half of this receipt. The mint was exported under `admit_callers` naming two witness wrappers, and because .dag HAS NO MODULE-PRIVATE each admitted wrapper was ITSELF an exported unrestricted constructor: a foreign module imported the wrapper, passed any digest it chose, and reached `ManifestRead` -- measured at 0 blocking errors, not argued. Sealing the wrapper in turn would only have moved the proxy one hop, because every helper that returns a value CARRYING the sealed type is the same escape, and the cascade terminates only at functions that carry nothing outward. THE CLASS ITSELF IS FILED SEPARATELY, at `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.admitted_call_edge_with_unrestricted_value_egress`, because it is about CONFINEMENT LEAKING THROUGH AN ADMITTED CALLER and applies to any sealed type, digest or not; restating its recognition rule here would be the meaning fork DESIGN section 3 forbids. It is cited, not repeated. THE REPAIR WAS TO DELETE THE FIXTURE ROUTE, not to fence it: the witnesses now obtain readings by digesting a committed fixture file through the one real constructor, which became possible on the required floor only after repairing `extdeps.crypto.hash` `crypto.Sha256Sum.File`'s mock_response -- it interpolated the input path into its canned line, the only such interpolation in any extdeps mock, so it refused on every hermetic call and had never once replayed. EVIDENCE STANDING, SEPARATED BY INSTRUMENT, because these prove different propositions and running them together is how a mocked route gets read as a measurement. The REQUIRED HERMETIC WITNESSES establish the modeled effect route and the verdict algebra USING THE MOCK; they do NOT establish that any real fixture bytes were hashed, and the now-working `mock_response` makes that route EXECUTABLE without making it a wet measurement. Only the hand-run instrument `tools.fabric_m0_origin_readback_probe` establishes that the live handler computes over real bytes, and no required lane runs it. The compile-refusal battery establishes a third thing -- what the acceptance path refuses -- and touches neither. That battery is `test.claim.fabric_m0_origin_readback_seal`: three forged-literal REDs (the reading, the commit carrier, the completion carrier), a fourth RED that is the exact source of the wrapper-proxy escape, kept as a permanent regression control (DESIGN section 4b(4)) and evidence that THE KNOWN ESCAPE REMAINS DELETED rather than that every imaginable proxy is absent, which no probe over one source can show, a green control on the sanctioned read route, and a DIFFERENTIAL rather than a bare zero. EVERY COUNT IS KEYED (class, subject_name, blocking) AND NOT BY CLASS ALONE: `gunbc.compile_diagnostic_census` states that CensusObserved carries every diagnostic the compile produced, the imported closure included, so a class-only count is attributable to the wrong subject and a class-only zero is a claim about the corpus. An earlier revision of that battery counted by class alone; the keyed form is the one the census module names as exact. A COMPILE REFUSAL SAYS NOTHING ABOUT WHETHER THE MINT DIGESTS THE FILE IT NAMES -- a mint returning a constant satisfies every one of those refusals -- so the other half runs against real bytes in the instrument `tools.fabric_m0_origin_readback_probe` `probe`: two committed fixture files digest to their own values and not to each other, an absent path refuses instead of producing a digest, and the whole commit gate publishes only on the recorded manifest. IT IS AN INSTRUMENT AND NOT A dag/test/claim WITNESS, AND THE REASON GENERALISES: under the hermetic envelope `crypto.Sha256Sum.File` replays ONE fixed mock digest for every path, so the discriminating arm is false by construction there -- and a `witness_exclusion_frontier` row does NOT save such a file, because `v2.workflow.required_floor` `required_floor_disposition_with_changed_selection` REPLACES the ordinary disposition, `DeclinedDiscoveryExcluded` included, with `PlannedAsChangedWitness` for any identity the change touches. That is deliberate: the rule own words are that the change is the moment its author is present to route it, rename it, or remove it. It was tried and the floor planned it anyway (gunbc#10934, run 34441858589), which is the executed evidence for this sentence. The honest routing is the third door, a named producer that re-derives the measurement on demand (DESIGN section 6), with its standing stated: no required lane executes it. RUNG -- AND FIRST A RETRACTION, because the sentence this replaces was merged and was not true. gunbc#10934 landed the claim that the sub-class was STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE `outside the enumerated admission`. THAT CLAIM WAS NOT ESTABLISHED AT THE HEAD THAT MERGED IT: the public wrapper route was open, so a foreign module could construct the state with no diagnostic, and between that merge and gunbc#10972 an INFLATED GUARANTEE STOOD ON MAIN. It is withdrawn here rather than adjusted, and gunbc#10972 RE-EARNS it by deleting the alternate mint -- the rung was never held in that interval, which is a different and worse thing than a rung that moved. DESIGN section 4b(1) is why this is written out instead of quietly corrected: an inflated class never ranks for climbing. THE RE-EARNED CLAIM IS THREE SEPARATE PROPOSITIONS and they must not be blurred. (i) THE DIGEST WAS COMPUTED OVER THE FILE THE READING NAMES -- structurally impossible to violate on the source-to-dag acceptance path, with the Rust-mirror caveat `gunbc.product.fabric.work` already records, because one operation produces both halves and no other constructor exists. (ii) THE NAMED FILE WAS THE ORIGIN OPERATION'S OWN OUTPUT -- MITIGATABLE, unchanged, never claimed higher. (iii) A CALLER-SUPPLIED CACHE PATH REMAINS POSSIBLE -- handing a /var/cache path to `read_origin_staged_file` yields an honest reading that confirms, and it must never be described as an origin read. Only (i) climbed. The ENCLOSING class -- was the digested file the origin readback own output rather than a cache copy -- stays MITIGATABLE, because `staged_path` is still chosen by the caller of the mint. Reporting the sub-class rung as the class rung would be the inflation section 4b(1) forbids. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, A CAPABILITY: a durable-origin read operation that STAGES ITS OWN OUTPUT at a path it mints, so the path a digest is computed over is produced by the read rather than chosen by its caller -- SUFFICIENT FOR removing caller-chosen paths from every confirming readback in fabric-M0, not merely for the commit manifest. It is declared once, at `gunbc.fabric_m0_origin_readback`, and cited from the two authorities rather than restated. THIS DOES NOT RETIRE THE ROW FIRST RECEIPT TRIGGER: that one names a carrier pairing a value with a digest derived at construction SUFFICIENT FOR every remaining peer-parameter signature in the corpus, and fabric-M0 is one population inside it.",
    "**A CORRECT DIGEST BESIDE A FALSE TIMESTAMP: THE SAME CLASS AT RECEIPT GRAIN, AND IT IS A SPECIMEN OF THIS ROW RATHER THAN A NEW IDENTITY (eager-owl-205, 2026-09-11).** Filing it as `integrity_mistaken_for_provenance` was considered and REJECTED: this row's scope is already a value beside a summary of it with no structural join, its recognition rule already says PROXIMITY TO A CORRECT THING IS NOT CORRECTNESS, and its fabric-M0 specimen already covers a correct digest beside a provenance stamp that can lie. A second identity would fork an owned meaning. INVALID STATE: a receipt declares an artifact's SHA-256 and byte count, and ALSO declares adjacent metadata ABOUT that artifact -- an observation timestamp, a subject identity, an attempt label -- with the digest joined to the bytes and the metadata joined to nothing. EVIDENCE, gunbc#10965: `mtcollins1_memory_census_observation` declared `mtcollins1_host_capture_observed_at` as 1789428410000, which is 2026-09-14T23:26:50Z, for a capture whose own first line reads `attempt=3 begin 2026-09-11T01:26:50Z` -- wrong by nearly four days -- while the declared digest a9b880c3... was CORRECT the entire time. A reader verifying the receipt the obvious way, by recomputing the hash, would have found it correct and the timestamp still false. THE SHARP STATEMENT, and it is the generalisable part: A CORRECT SHA-256 AUTHENTICATES THOSE BYTES, NOT ADJACENT METADATA ABOUT THOSE BYTES. The digest's scope is exactly the byte sequence; every other field in the receipt is an independent assertion wearing the digest's credibility. SECOND SPECIMEN IN THE SAME MODULE, which is what makes it a class rather than one slip: the same receipt carried `attempt=3` as the identity of BOTH a failed run that produced no host capture and the successful run that produced these bytes, because the capture script hard-codes its attempt number and a rerun re-used it. The artifact FILE was correctly never overwritten -- the rule that was being followed -- and the identity INSIDE it was, which is the same failure one level down. Any receipt join keyed on that label would merge a failure with a success. DISTINGUISHING FACTS: both defects were authored by the same hand that authored the correct digest, in the same commit, and neither is detectable by any check that verifies digests; the pure witness over that module could not have caught either, because a `.dag` witness has no file access and can only compare declarations to declarations. RECOGNITION RULE, specialised to receipts: when a receipt carries a digest, ask which of its OTHER fields were derived from the digested bytes and which were typed by an author. Every field in the second set is unverified input sitting inside a structure that reads as verified. REPAIR, consistent with this row's declared one: DERIVE THE METADATA FROM THE ARTIFACT AT THE POINT THE ARTIFACT IS PRODUCED. `extdeps.tools.sha256sum` `sha256sum_file_digest_via_shell` already computes over a path, and `gunbc.fabric_m0_origin_readback` `OriginReading` is this row's existing pattern for pairing path and computed digest in one operation; the receipt-grain version is a producer that writes the artifact and returns its byte count, digest AND observation window together, so none of them can name a different capture. RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable -- caught by human review, after landing, twice. CEILING: structurally impossible for the derivable fields, via a producer-minted receipt. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a capability: a capture producer that GENERATES the receipt from the artifact it just wrote -- SUFFICIENT FOR byte count, digest, observation window and attempt identity simultaneously, which is what distinguishes it from repairing any one field. The affected receipt's fields were corrected by hand and the acquisition standing marked `HistoricalAdHocCapture { reusable_producer_absent: true }` so the observations cannot masquerade as evidence that such a producer exists.)",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
